      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate carefully.\n\n**Certificate-level info:**\n- Number: R49/2013-A-CZ1-24.02\n- Revision 3\n- Scheme: A\n- Member State: Czech Republic\n- Issuing Authority: Czech Metrology Institute, Okružní 31, 638 00 Brno, Czech Republic\n- Person responsible: Jan Kalandra\n- Applicant: GWF AG, Obergrundstrasse 119, CH-6005 Luzern, Switzerland\n- Manufacturer: GWF AG, same address\n- Certified type: Water meter – ultrasonic, dry dial; sonico NANO\n- Module designation: \"-\" (dash means not applicable → \"Not applicable\" or null)\n- Recommendation: OIML R 49, Edition 2013\n- Accuracy class: 2\n- Date issued: 11 March 2026 (Revision 3 date; the date of signature)\n- Page total: 10\n- Project number: not clearly present. Hmm, there's \"0511-ER-V124-25\" etc. but no explicit project number. Omit.\n- OIML issuer id: not present explicitly. There's \"V 3112\" which is possibly a mark. I'll omit.\n\n**Issuing authority:**\n- name: Czech Metrology Institute\n- address_lines: [\"Okružní 31\", \"638 00 Brno\", \"Czech Republic\"]\n- person_responsible: Jan Kalandra\n- On page 2: \"The OIML Issuing Authority: RNDr. Pavel Klenovský, Head of Certification Body\" — that's the signer. person_responsible is Jan Kalandra per page 1. The signer is Pavel Klenovský. I could add person_title: \"Head of Certification Body\" — but that title belongs to Klenovský. The schema has person_responsible and person_title. I'll put person_responsible: \"Jan Kalandra\" and maybe not the title since it applies to Klenovský. Actually maybe I can note both. Schema fields: name, address_lines[], person_responsible, person_title, phone, email, website, oiml_issuer_id. I'll set person_responsible: \"Jan Kalandra\". The signature block person is RNDr. Pavel Klenovský, Head of Certification Body. Hmm, ambiguous. I'll keep person_responsible as Jan Kalandra (explicitly labeled \"Person responsible\"). Person title could be omitted or set to \"Head of Certification Body\". I'll omit person_title to avoid misattribution... Actually the schema allows it; but it'd be wrong attribution. I'll omit.\n\n**Applicants:** GWF AG, Obergrundstrasse 119, CH-6005 Luzern, Switzerland\n\n**Manufacturers:** GWF AG, same\n\n**Certified type:**\n- category: \"Water meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"sonico NANO\"] — note also \"sonico Nano\" in the characteristics table; it's the same. I'll include \"sonico NANO\".\n- module_designation: the certificate says \"-\" → \"Not applicable\" (per schema: \"Not applicable\" | null). The dash indicates not applicable. I'll put null or \"Not applicable\". I'll use \"Not applicable\".\n\nHmm, actually the module designation says \"-\" which means not applicable. But wait — there's mention of \"NB-IoT communication module\" added in revision 3. That's a communication module not certified itself. The \"Designation of the module (if applicable)\" being \"-\" suggests no module per se. I'll use null. Actually, schema says module_designation: \"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null. I'll go with \"Not applicable\".\n\n- description: \"Ultrasonic water meter with dry dial... designed to measure, memorise and display the volume at metering conditions of water passing through the measurement transducer. Cast brass body with connecting screw threads, one pair of ultrasonic transducers and electronic indicating device with LCD display...\"\n\n**Model family:**\nFamily name: \"sonico NANO\". Models: nominal diameters 15, 20, 25, 32, 40. But actually the model axis is two-dimensional: DN (nominal diameter) × Q3 (flowrate) × ratio Q3/Q1. The flowrate tables give variants per DN for Q3 = 1.6/2.5 (DN15), 2.5/4.0 (DN20), 6.3/10 (DN25, DN32), 10/16 (DN40), with ratios 40–1000 (DN15 up to 1000, DN20 up to 1000, DN25 up to 1000, DN32 up to 1000, DN40 up to 1000).\n\nHow to model? Options:\n- models[] with model_id like \"DN15 Q3=1.6 R40\" etc. That would be ~5 DNs × 2 Q3 × many ratios. That's a lot.\n- Simpler: models[] per DN: DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32, DN40, with attributes: q3_values (multiple), ratio range.\n\nGiven the instruction \"include ONLY if cert covers >1 model variant\" — yes. The main axis is nominal diameter. I'll create models for DN15..DN40, with attributes like {nominal_diameter_mm: X}. Then model_level characteristics keyed on those model ids: e.g., \"q3\" values lists, pressure_loss_class per DN, connection type per DN, length per DN.\n\nActually the pressure loss class row gives 5 values aligned to the 5 DNs:\n- DN15: Q3 1.6 Δp10 / Q3 2.5 Δp16\n- DN20: Q3 2.5 Δp16 / Q3 4.0 Δp25\n- DN25: Q3 6.3 Δp25 / Q3 10 Δp40\n- DN32: Q3 6.3 Δp16 / Q3 10 Δp25\n- DN40: Q3 10 Δp10 / Q3 16 Δp16\n\nConnection type row has only 4 cells for 5 columns (probably the OCR merged DN15 and DN20? No — looks like 4 cells: \"NPSM or G type ¾\", 7/8\", 1\"\" ; \"NPSM or G type 7/8\", 1\", 1 1/4\"\" ; \"NPSM or G type 1 1/4\", 1 ½\"\" ; \"NPSM or G type 2\"\". Hmm 4 cells for 5 DNs. Likely mapping: DN15: ¾\",7/8\",1\"; DN20: 7/8\",1\",1 1/4\"; DN25: 1 1/4\",1 ½\"; DN32: ? DN40: 2\". Ambiguous. Actually possibly DN15 and DN20 share? The header row had 5 nominal diameters: 15,20,25,32,40. The connection row shows 4 cells. Could be: DN15: ¾,7/8,1; DN20: 7/8,1,1¼; DN25: 1¼,1½; DN32: 1½? missing; DN40: 2. It's ambiguous — I should record it cautiously. I'll include connection types in matrix_tables as-is with a description noting the OCR ambiguity, or attribute with values per DN where mapping is uncertain. Safer: record the connection types per the cells as they appear in a matrix_table \"installation_details\" with columns and rows, noting alignment uncertainty. Hmm.\n\nLength row: ≥105, ≥105, ≥150, ≥150, ≥150 for the 5 DNs — clean.\n\nType-level characteristics (constant across models):\n- measuring_principle: \"ultrasonic\"\n- accuracy_class: 2\n- mpe_lower_zone: ±5 % → value 5, unit \"%\", or string \"±5 %\"? Better: value: {min: -5, max: 5}? Hmm. The convention: MPE ±5%. I'd record as value: 5, unit \"%\", with maybe a note. Or keep string \"±5\". Rule 2 says numbers not strings. For ±5% I could do {min: -5, max: 5}. I think value: 5 with unit \"%\" is fine but loses sign. I'll use {min: -5, max: 5}, unit \"%\". Hmm, that might be over-interpretation. Many extractors use \"±5\". I'll go with value: 5, unit_symbol: \"%\" and label mpe_lower_zone... Actually to be safe and precise: \"value\": {\"min\": -5, \"max\": 5}. I'll do that.\n- q2_over_q1: 1.6\n- q4_over_q3: 1.25\n- temperature_class: \"T50\"\n- pressure_class: \"MAP16\" (water pressure class)\n- reverse_flow: \"designed to measure\"\n- mechanical_environment_class: \"B, O, M\"\n- electromagnetic_environment_class: \"E2\"\n- temperature_range_ambient: -25 to 70 °C → {min: -25, max: 70}, unit \"°C\"\n- maximum_admissible_temperature: 50 °C\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa\n- orientation: \"any\"\n- indicating_range_testing_mode: 9 999 m³ → 9999\n- indicating_range_user_mode: 999 999 m³ → 999999\n- resolution_indicating_device_testing_mode: 0.00001 m³\n- resolution_indicating_device_user_mode: 0.001 m³\n- resolution_device_rapid_testing: 100 pulse/dm³\n- resolution_indicating_device_rapid_testing: 0.000001 m³\n- EUT category: \"Ultrasonic water meters\"; case: \"B\"\n- power supply type: \"Non replaceable battery\" (battery, 3.6 V)\n- umax: 3.6 V\n- umin: 1.9 V\n- minimum_battery_life: 16 years\n- software versions: \"0.6.28; 1.0.1; 2.7.2; 3.2.2\"\n- CRC checksums: list of hex strings\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0D0\"\n- flow_conditioner: \"No\"\n- min straight lengths: 0 mm both\n- installation: orientation any\n\nModel-level (varying by DN):\n- nominal_diameter: 15/20/25/32/40 mm\n- q3: values per DN: DN15: 1.6 or 2.5; DN20: 2.5 or 4.0; DN25: 6.3 or 10; DN32: 6.3 or 10; DN40: 10 or 16 (m³/h)\n- pressure_loss_class per DN (string like \"Δp10/Δp16\" with Q3 combos)\n- connection_type per DN (uncertain mapping)\n- minimum_length: {min:105} mm for DN15, DN20; {min:150} for DN25, DN32, DN40\n\nAlso Q3/Q1 ratios per DN (footnote ¹: \"The ratio Q₃ / Q₁ shall be chosen according to paragraph 4.1.4 of OIML R 49-1:2013\"):\n- DN15: ratios 40–1000 (40,50,63,80,100,125,160,200,250,315,400,500,630,800,1000)\n- DN20: 40–1000\n- DN25: 40–1000\n- DN32: 40–1000\n- DN40: 40–1000\n\nWait: Revision 3 change: \"Addition of ratio for water meter DN25 from R630 to R1000\". Hmm, DN25 third table has ratios 630, 800, 1000. And DN25 Q1 values: 0.010, 0.016, 0.008, 0.013, 0.006, 0.010 with Q3 6.30/10.00 alternating. Note the Q3/Q1 row: colspan 2 → 630, colspan 2 → 800, colspan 2 → 1000? Actually for DN25 last table: 6 columns, Q3/Q1: 630 (span 2), 800 (span 2), 1000 (span 2). But Q1 values: 0.010, 0.016 (Q3 6.30/10.00 → ratio 630/400? wait 6.3/0.010=630, 10/0.016=625? Hmm 10.00/0.016=625 ≈ 630? Actually 630 would give 0.01587. They use 0.016. Fine.)\n\nHmm wait, for DN25 table: Q3/Q1 = 630 for columns 1-2? But Q1 for col2 = 0.016 and Q3 = 10.00 → 625. OK approximate; use labeled ratio.\n\nLet me carefully parse each flow table to build matrix_tables.\n\n**Flow tables:**\n\nTable A (DN15, ratios 40–125): 12 value columns.\n- Q1: 0.040, 0.063, 0.032, 0.050, 0.025, 0.040, 0.020, 0.031, 0.016, 0.025, 0.013, 0.020\n- Q2: 0.064, 0.100, 0.051, 0.080, 0.041, 0.064, 0.032, 0.050, 0.026, 0.040, 0.021, 0.032\n- Q3: 1.600, 2.500 alternating\n- Q4: 2.000, 3.125 alternating\n- Q3/Q1: 40 (cols1-2), 50 (cols3-4), 63 (cols5-6), 80 (cols7-8), 100 (cols9-10), 125 (cols11-12)\n\nSo each ratio has two Q3 variants (1.6 and 2.5):\n- ratio 40: (Q3=1.6, Q1=0.040), (Q3=2.5, Q1=0.063)\n- ratio 50: (1.6, 0.032), (2.5, 0.050)\n- ratio 63: (1.6, 0.025), (2.5, 0.040)\n- ratio 80: (1.6, 0.020), (2.5, 0.031)\n- ratio 100: (1.6, 0.016), (2.5, 0.025)\n- ratio 125: (1.6, 0.013), (2.5, 0.020)\n\nQ2 = 1.6 × Q1: 0.064, 0.100(0.1008), 0.051, 0.080, 0.041(0.041), 0.064, 0.032, 0.050, 0.026, 0.040, 0.021(0.0208), 0.032. Yes.\n\nTable B (DN15, ratios 160–500): \n- Q1: 0.010, 0.016, 0.008, 0.013, 0.006, 0.010, 0.005, 0.008, 0.004, 0.006, 0.003, 0.005\n- Q2: 0.016, 0.025, 0.013, 0.020, 0.010, 0.016, 0.008, 0.013, 0.006, 0.010, 0.005, 0.008\n- Q3: 1.600/2.500 alternating; Q4: 2.000/3.125\n- ratios: 160, 200, 250, 315, 400, 500\n\nTable C (DN15, ratios 630–1000): 4 columns\n- Q1: 0.0025, 0.004, 0.003, 0.0025\n- Q2: 0.004, 0.006, 0.005, 0.004\n- Q3: 1.600, 2.500, 2.500, 2.500\n- Q4: 2.000, 3.125, 3.125, 3.125\n- Q3/Q1: 630 (span 2), 800, 1000\n\nSo ratio 630: (1.6, 0.0025), (2.5, 0.004); ratio 800: (2.5, 0.003); ratio 1000: (2.5, 0.0025).\n\nTable D (DN20, ratios 40–125): 12 cols\n- Q1: 0.063, 0.100, 0.050, 0.080, 0.040, 0.064, 0.031, 0.050, 0.025, 0.040, 0.020, 0.032\n- Q2: 0.100, 0.160, 0.080, 0.128, 0.064, 0.102, 0.050, 0.080, 0.040, 0.064, 0.032, 0.051\n- Q3: 2.500/4.00; Q4: 3.125/5.00\n- ratios: 40,50,63,80,100,125\n\nTable E (DN20, ratios 160–500):\n- Q1: 0.016, 0.025, 0.013, 0.020, 0.010, 0.016, 0.008, 0.013, 0.006, 0.010, 0.005, 0.008\n- Q2: 0.025, 0.040, 0.020, 0.032, 0.016, 0.026, 0.013, 0.020, 0.010, 0.016, 0.008, 0.013\n- Q3: 2.500/4.00; Q4: 3.125/5.00\n- ratios: 160..500\n\nTable F (DN20, ratios 630–1000): 4 cols\n- Q1: 0.004, 0.006, 0.005, 0.004\n- Q2: 0.006, 0.010, 0.008, 0.006\n- Q3: 2.500, 4.00, 4.00, 4.00\n- Q4: 3.125, 5.00, 5.00, 5.00\n- ratios: 630 (span2), 800, 1000\n\nTable G (DN25, ratios 40–125): 12 cols\n- Q1: 0.156, 0.250, 0.126, 0.200, 0.100, 0.159, 0.080, 0.125, 0.063, 0.100, 0.050, 0.080\n- Q2: 0.252, 0.400, 0.202, 0.320, 0.160, 0.254, 0.126, 0.200, 0.101, 0.160, 0.080, 0.128\n- Q3: 6.30/10.00; Q4: 7.875/12.50\n- ratios: 40..125\n\nTable H (DN25, ratios 160–500):\n- Q1: 0.039, 0.063, 0.032, 0.050, 0.025, 0.040, 0.020, 0.032, 0.016, 0.025, 0.013, 0.020\n- Q2: 0.063, 0.100, 0.050, 0.080, 0.040, 0.064, 0.032, 0.051, 0.025, 0.040, 0.020, 0.032\n- Q3: 6.30/10.00; Q4: 7.875/12.50\n- ratios: 160..500\n\nTable I (DN25, ratios 630–1000): 6 cols\n- Q1: 0.010, 0.016, 0.008, 0.013, 0.006, 0.010\n- Q2: 0.016, 0.025, 0.013, 0.020, 0.010, 0.016\n- Q3: 6.30, 10.00 alternating\n- Q4: 7.875, 12.50\n- ratios: 630 (span2), 800 (span2), 1000 (span2)\n\nTable J (DN32, ratios 40–125): same as G (Q1 identical): 0.156, 0.250, ... Q3 6.30/10.00\nTable K (DN32, ratios 160–500): same as H\nTable L (DN32, ratios 630–1000): 4 cols\n- Q1: 0.010, 0.016, 0.013, 0.010\n- Q2: 0.016, 0.025, 0.020, 0.016\n- Q3: 6.30, 10.00, 10.00, 10.00\n- Q4: 7.875, 12.50, 12.50, 12.50\n- ratios: 630 (span2), 800, 1000\n\nTable M (DN40, ratios 40–125):\n- Q1: 0.250, 0.400, 0.200, 0.320, 0.159, 0.254, 0.125, 0.200, 0.100, 0.160, 0.080, 0.128\n- Q2: 0.400, 0.640, 0.320, 0.512, 0.254, 0.406, 0.200, 0.320, 0.160, 0.256, 0.128, 0.205\n- Q3: 10.00/16.0; Q4: 12.50/20.0\n- ratios 40..125\n\nTable N (DN40, ratios 160–500):\n- Q1: 0.063, 0.100, 0.050, 0.080, 0.040, 0.064, 0.032, 0.051, 0.025, 0.040, 0.020, 0.032\n- Q2: 0.100, 0.160, 0.080, 0.128, 0.064, 0.102, 0.051, 0.081, 0.040, 0.064, 0.032, 0.051\n- Q3: 10.00/16.0; Q4: 12.50/20.0\n- ratios 160..500\n\nTable O (DN40, ratios 630–1000): 4 cols\n- Q1: 0.016, 0.024, 0.020, 0.016\n- Q2: 0.025, 0.041, 0.032, 0.026\n- Q3: 10.00, 16.0, 16.0, 16.0\n- Q4: 12.50, 20.0, 20.0, 20.0\n- ratios: 630 (span2), 800, 1000\n\nNow, how to represent? The schema wants matrix_tables for multi-dim tables, plus model_family and model_level characteristics. The full expansion would be 15 ratios × ~2 Q3 × 5 DN = huge (~120+ variants). That's too much for models[] entries individually? The example R137 with 2 sizes did full expansion. Here we have ~15 ratios × 2 Q3 × 5 DN = 150 combos... Actually let me count: DN15: 6×2 + 6×2 + 2 + 1 + 1 = 12+12+4 = wait table C has 4 columns → 4 variants. So DN15: 12+12+4 = 28. Same DN20: 28, DN25: 12+12+6=30, DN32: 12+12+4=28, DN40: 12+12+4=28. Total ~142 variants. Too many to list individually as models.\n\nPragmatic approach: model_family.models = per DN (5 models: DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32, DN40), with attributes describing Q3 options and ratio range. matrix_tables: represent the flowrate data compactly. For m