15 28, DN20 28, DN25 28, DN32 28, DN40 28 = 140. DN25: 12+12+4? No—DN25 last table has 6 columns: ratio 630 ×2, 800 ×1, 1000 ×1 → 12+12+4=28. Yes 140 rows.)\n\nCheck DN25 ratio 800: Q1 = 0.008, Q3 = 10 → 10/0.008 = 1250?? Wait: 800 → Q1 = 10/800 = 0.0125 ≈ 0.013. Yes table I col3: Q1 0.008? Let me recheck DN25 table I: Q1: 0.010, 0.016, 0.008, 0.013, 0.006, 0.010; Q3: 6.30, 10.00, 6.30, 10.00, 6.30, 10.00; ratios: 630(span2), 800(span2), 1000(span2). Hmm with 6 columns and ratios spanning 2 each: cols1-2 = 630, cols3-4 = 800, cols5-6 = 1000.\n\ncol1: Q3 6.3, Q1 0.010 → 630 ✓\ncol2: Q3 10.0, Q1 0.016 → 625 (nominal 630, OK since Q1 rounded up from 0.01587)\ncol3: Q3 6.3, Q1 0.008 → 787.5 → ratio 800 ✓\ncol4: Q3 10.0, Q1 0.013 → 769 → 800 ✓ (0.0125→0.013)\ncol5: Q3 6.3, Q1 0.006 → 1050 → 1000 ✓ (0.0063→0.006)\ncol6: Q3 10.0, Q1 0.010 → 1000 ✓\n\nGood. So DN25: ratio 800 has only ONE variant? col3 is Q3 6.3 (ratio 800) and col4 is Q3 10.0 (ratio 800). Both are ratio 800! So ratio 800: (6.3, 0.008), (10.0, 0.013). Ratio 1000: (6.3, 0.006), (10.0, 0.010). Wait but ratio 630 cols1-2 = (6.3, 0.010), (10.0, 0.016).\n\nHmm! I misread earlier. Let me redo: the 6-column DN25 table: Q3/Q1 row: 630 colspan2, 800 colspan2, 1000 colspan2. Columns:\n1: Q1=0.010, Q3=6.30 → ratio 630\n2: Q1=0.016, Q3=10.00 → 625≈630\n3: Q1=0.008, Q3=6.30 → 787.5≈800\n4: Q1=0.013, Q3=10.00 → 769≈800\n5: Q1=0.006, Q3=6.30 → 1050≈1000\n6: Q1=0.010, Q3=10.00 → 1000\n\nSo DN25 ratio 800: two variants (6.3 & 10.0); ratio 1000: two variants (6.3 & 10.0). So DN25 has 12+12+6 = 30 rows. OK so my original count of 30 was right; rows 81-84 need fixing:\n\n81. {25, 630, 6.3, 7.875, 0.010, 0.016}\n82. {25, 630, 10.0, 12.5, 0.016, 0.025}\n83. {25, 800, 6.3, 7.875, 0.008, 0.013}\n84. {25, 800, 10.0, 12.5, 0.013, 0.020}\n84b. {25, 1000, 6.3, 7.875, 0.006, 0.010}\n84c. {25, 1000, 10.0, 12.5, 0.010, 0.016}\n\nWait, earlier I wrote DN25 row 83 as {25, 800, 10.0, 0.008} — wrong. Correct per table: col3 has Q3=6.30, Q1=0.008. And revision note says \"Addition of ratio for water meter DN25 from R630 to R1000\" — consistent: 630, 800, 1000 all new for DN25.\n\nSimilarly DN15 table C (4 cols): Q3: 1.600, 2.500, 2.500, 2.500; ratios 630(span2), 800, 1000:\ncol1: 1.6, 0.0025 → 640≈630 ✓\ncol2: 2.5, 0.004 → 625≈630 ✓\ncol3: 2.5, 0.003 → 833≈800 ✓\ncol4: 2.5, 0.0025 → 1000 ✓\nSo DN15: ratio 630: (1.6, 0.0025), (2.5, 0.004); ratio 800: (2.5, 0.003); ratio 1000: (2.5, 0.0025). 28 rows ✓ (my rows 25-28 correct).\n\nDN20 table F: Q3: 2.500, 4.00, 4.00, 4.00; ratios 630(2), 800, 1000:\ncol1: 2.5, 0.004 → 625≈630\ncol2: 4.0, 0.006 → 667≈630\ncol3: 4.0, 0.005 → 800 ✓\ncol4: 4.0, 0.004 → 1000 ✓\n28 rows ✓.\n\nDN32 table L: Q3: 6.30, 10.00, 10.00, 10.00; ratios 630(2), 800, 1000:\ncol1: 6.3, 0.010 → 630 ✓\ncol2: 10.0, 0.016 → 625≈630\ncol3: 10.0, 0.013 → 769≈800\ncol4: 10.0, 0.010 → 1000\n28 rows ✓.\n\nDN40 table O: Q3: 10.00, 16.0, 16.0, 16.0:\ncol1: 10, 0.016 → 625≈630\ncol2: 16, 0.024 → 667≈630\ncol3: 16, 0.020 → 800 ✓\ncol4: 16, 0.016 → 1000 ✓\n28 rows ✓.\n\nTotal: 28×4 + 30 = 142 rows. OK, 142.\n\nHmm, that's a lot. Should I really enumerate all 142 rows? The schema's matrix_tables preserves multi-dim tables. It's the most faithful. But output length... each row ~75 chars → ~11KB. Plus rest ~8KB. ~20KB JSON. Acceptable.\n\nAlternatively, compress: since q2 = 1.6·q1 exactly (rounded), and q4 = 1.25·q3, I could omit q2 and q4? No, keep fidelity.\n\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider: maybe I should keep q2 and q4. Yes.\n\nNow decide model_level characteristics (beyond the matrix table):\n\n- nominal_diameter: values per model DN15..40: 15,20,25,32,40 mm\n- q3: values per model: {min:1.6,max:2.5} etc. — I'll do attribute \"q3\" unit m³/h with {min,max} representing the two available nominal flowrates per DN. Hmm, is that misleading? The two options are discrete. Using min/max of the set is a compact representation. I'll add \"value\": {\"min\": 1.6, \"max\": 2.5}. Acceptable.\n\nActually, maybe better attribute name \"permanent_flowrate_q3\". R49 uses Q3 as permanent flowrate. Label canonical: \"q3\" or \"permanent_flowrate_q3\". I'll use \"q3\".\n\n- pressure_loss_class: per DN strings:\n  - DN15: \"Q3 1.6 Δp10 / Q3 2.5 Δp16\"\n  - DN20: \"Q3 2.5 Δp16 / Q3 4.0 Δp25\"\n  - DN25: \"Q3 6.3 Δp25 / Q3 10 Δp40\"\n  - DN32: \"Q3 6.3 Δp16 / Q3 10 Δp25\"\n  - DN40: \"Q3 10 Δp10 / Q3 16 Δp16\"\n  Value as string. Fine.\n\n- minimum_length: DN15 {min:105,max:null} mm; DN20 same; DN25/32/40 {min:150}.\n\n- Also maybe \"q3_over_q1_available_range\": per DN 40–1000. Add attribute \"q3_over_q1\": values {min:40, max:1000} per DN with footnote marker 1. Good.\n\nmodel_level entries: nominal_diameter_mm, q3, q3_over_q1_range, pressure_loss_class, minimum_length. Also connection types? Skip due to ambiguity; keep in matrix table.\n\nActually wait — also \"q4\" per DN: DN15 {2,3.125}, etc. Skip; in matrix.\n\nAlso \"installation_connection_type\" — I'll put into a second matrix_table \"Installation details\" with columns [nominal_diameter, connection_type, length_mm] rows:\n- 15: \"NPSM or G type ¾\\\", 7/8\\\", 1\\\"\" ; 105\n- 20: \"NPSM or G type 7/8\\\", 1\\\", 1 1/4\\\"\" ; 105\n- 25: \"NPSM or G type 1 1/4\\\", 1 1/2\\\"\" ; 150\n- 32: null ; 150\n- 40: \"NPSM or G type 2\\\"\" ; 150\nwith description noting OCR merged/4 cells for 5 columns and alignment of the last two cells (DN32/DN40) uncertain.\n\nHmm, including null for DN32 — fine.\n\nQuote characters: inches mark \" inside JSON strings needs escaping \\\". The source uses ¾”, 7/8”, 1” (curly quotes ”). I'll use the curly right-double-quote character ” directly (U+201D) which doesn't need escaping. Good: \"NPSM or G type ¾”, 7/8”, 1”\".\n\nAlso earlier cells: \"NPSM or G type  1 1/4”, 1 ½”\". Keep.\n\n**Type-level characteristics list (final):**\n\ntype_level:\n- measuring_principle: \"ultrasonic\"\n- accuracy_class: 2 (value 2, unit null)\n- mpe_lower_flowrate_zone: {min:-5, max:5} \"%\"\n- mpe_upper_flowrate_zone: {min:-2, max:2} \"%\"\n- q2_over_q1: 1.6\n- q4_over_q3: 1.25\n- temperature_class: \"T50\"\n- water_pressure_class: \"MAP16\"\n- reverse_flow: \"designed to measure\"\n- mechanical_environment_class: \"B, O, M\"\n- electromagnetic_environment_class: \"E2\"\n- ambient_temperature_range: {min:-25, max:70} \"°C\"\n- maximum_admissible_temperature: 50 \"°C\"\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 \"MPa\"\n- orientation_limitation: \"any\"\n- indicating_range_testing_mode: 9999 \"m³\"\n- indicating_range_user_mode: 999999 \"m³\"\n- resolution_indicating_device_testing_mode: 0.00001 \"m³\"\n- resolution_indicating_device_user_mode: 0.001 \"m³\"\n- resolution_rapid_testing_device: 100 \"pulse/dm³\"\n- resolution_indicating_device_rapid_testing: 0.000001 \"m³\"\n- eut_test_category: \"Ultrasonic water meters\"\n- eut_test_case: \"B\"\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0D0\"\n- flow_conditioner_required: \"No\"\n- min_straight_length_inlet_pipe: 0 mm\n- min_straight_length_outlet_pipe: 0 mm\n- power_supply_type: \"Non replaceable battery\"\n- battery_voltage_max: 3.6 V\n- battery_voltage_min: 1.9 V\n- minimum_battery_life: 16 \"years\"\n- software_version: [\"0.6.28\",\"1.0.1\",\"2.7.3\"?] — string list. The value shape: value can be string or list? Schema says value: number | {min,max} | string. Multiple SW versions — keep as string \"0.6.28; 1.0.1; 2.7.2; 3.2.2\". OK.\n- crc_checksum: \"0x854EBACF, 0x13372073, 0x3A681C19, 0x1d901C40, 0x3F790676, 0x0AB7802F, 0x01F76A0B\" — keep as string with the original. Keep original casing: \"0x854EBACF, 0x13372073; 0x3A681C19, 0x1d901C40; 0x3F790676; 0x0AB7802F; 0x01F76A0B\". Fine.\n- software_type: \"Type P embedded software\" — extensions I1, S, D. Record as software_extensions: \"I1 (Water meters), S (Software separation), D (Download of Legally Relevant Software)\" string.\n\nVolume resolution description also says \"displays volume resolution of 0.00001 m³\" — covered.\n\nValue structure: each must be {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}? The schema type_level: <label>: {value, unit, footnote_markers[]}. Looking at schema yaml: `<label>: {value, unit, footnote_markers[]}`. But rule 3 shows structured values with `unit_symbol` and `unit_id`. Hmm, schema shows \"unit\" but rules show unit_symbol/unit_id. The R137 example model_level uses \"unit_symbol\". I'll use {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers} consistently (rule 3 STRICT shape). unit_id: null everywhere.\n\n**model_family:**\nfamily_name: \"sonico NANO\"\nmodels: [\n {model_id: \"DN15\", attributes: {nominal_diameter: 15}},\n {model_id: \"DN20\", attributes: {nominal_diameter: 20}},\n {model_id: \"DN25\", attributes: {nominal_diameter: 25}},\n {model_id: \"DN32\", attributes: {nominal_diameter: 32}},\n {model_id: \"DN40\", attributes: {nominal_diameter: 40}}\n]\n\n**Components:**\n[\n {role: \"measuring transducer\", type_designations: [\"pair of ultrasonic transducers\"], characteristics: null},\n {role: \"electronic indicating device\", type_designations: [\"LCD display\"], ...},\n {role: \"communication module (optional, not certified)\", type_designations: [\"NB-IoT module\"]},\n {role: \"impulse module (optional, not certified)\", type_designations: null}\n]\nKeep simple; characteristics maybe volume resolution. I'll include minimal characteristics or omit. Schema components have role, type_designations, alternatives, characteristics{}. I'll include some.\n\nActually maybe better to keep components minimal:\n- {role: \"measurement transducer\", type_designations: [\"ultrasonic transducers (one pair)\"]}\n- {role: \"calculating and indicating device\", type_designations: [\"electronic indicating device with LCD display\"]}\n- {role: \"communication module (not certified, no influence on metrological parameters)\", type_designations: [\"NB-IoT module\"], alternatives: null}\n\n**Test reports:** dates only for the ER; test report dates not given individually. pages: numbers. role fields.\n\n- {id: \"0511-ER-V12